This is a clear model to build from. The core unit is a 1:1 handshake between two apps: I test your app, you test mine. Here is how I’d structure your platform around that.

1. Key rules
One shared Google Group. Every member joins it once, and every developer adds its address to their closed testing track. Consumer Google Groups can’t add members through an API, so set the group to “anyone can join” (or “ask to join” plus your approval) and show the join link during onboarding.
Fixed campaign. Each campaign has 16 tester slots and runs 16 days. Google needs 12 testers opted in for 14 continuous days, so this gives 4 spare testers and 2 spare days.
Handshake. You request to test someone’s app and offer one of your own apps in return. Once they accept, both of you join each other’s test.
Slots by level. Your level caps how many handshakes you can have active at once (12 to 20). Completing handshakes raises your level.
Penalty. If you skip your half of a handshake, you’re blocked from new handshakes for a while.
Matching filter. You only see apps whose minimum Android version is at or below your device’s version.
2. Database structure
users
  id, google_email (must match Play account), name
  device_android_api        -- e.g. 26 = Android 8.0
  level, completed_handshakes, failed_handshakes
  reliability_score, blocked_until
  joined_google_group (bool), created_at

apps
  id, owner_id, name, package_name
  join_android_link, join_web_link, logo_url
  min_android_api
  group_added_confirmed (bool), countries_all_confirmed (bool)
  tester_slots (16), duration_days (16)
  status: draft | in_review | open | full | completed | archived
  created_at

handshakes
  id
  user_a_id, app_a_id      -- requester and the app they offer
  user_b_id, app_b_id      -- target owner and their app
  status: requested | accepted | rejected | active | completed | broken | cancelled
  requested_at, accepted_at, started_at, ends_at

handshake_sides            -- 2 rows per handshake, one per direction
  id, handshake_id
  tester_id, app_id        -- who is testing which app
  joined_at, optin_proof_url
  last_checkin_date, checkin_count, missed_streak
  status: pending | joined | active | completed | skipped

daily_checkins
  side_id, date, proof_url (optional), note

feedback
  side_id, rating, bugs, comments, created_at

levels
  level, active_slots, completions_required

blocks
  user_id, reason, handshake_id, starts_at, ends_at

Splitting each handshake into two sides is the important design choice. A can do their part perfectly while B skips. With sides, you penalize only B and can still reward A.

3. Level system (example values)
Level	Active slots	Completed handshakes needed
1	12	0
2	14	10
3	16	25
4	18	50
5	20	100

Before creating or accepting a request, check that the user’s active handshakes are below their level’s slot limit.

4. Matching logic

Discover feed for user U shows apps where:

the owner is not U
the app’s status is “open” (fewer than 16 accepted handshakes)
the app’s minimum Android version is at or below U’s device version
U has no existing handshake with this app
the owner is not blocked

Sort by owner reliability, then fewest testers so far, so new apps fill up quickly.

Sending a request: U picks one of their own apps to offer. That app must be open, must not have been tested by the target owner before, and its minimum Android version must be at or below the target owner’s device version.

Accepting: check both users’ free slots and both apps’ free tester slots in a single database transaction, so two people can’t take the last slot at the same time.

5. Handshake lifecycle
Requested. The target owner gets a notification and can accept or reject. Auto-expire the request after 48 hours.
Accepted. Both sides get the other app’s join link. Each must opt in and upload a screenshot of the “You’re a tester” page within, say, 48 hours.
Active. Once both sides have joined, the 16-day clock starts. Each side checks in daily (“opened today”).
Daily cron job:
Increase missed_streak for anyone who didn’t check in yesterday.
At 2 missed days, send a warning.
At 3 or more, mark that side as skipped, mark the handshake as broken, block the skipper (e.g. 7 days, longer for repeat offenders), and lower their reliability score.
The innocent side keeps their completion credit, and their app gets priority in the discover feed to find a replacement tester.
Completed. Both sides reach day 16 with no skip. Increase each user’s completed count, recheck their level, and let them uninstall.
6. Onboarding and app submission flow

Mirror the 4 steps you showed, as a checklist that blocks submission until done:

Join our Google Group (button opens the join link). The user ticks a confirmation box.
Prepare in Play Console. The user confirms they added the group email to the closed track’s testers, selected all countries, and sent the release for review.
App details:
Paste the testing link. Validate it with a regex and extract the package name. The “Join on web” link looks like play.google.com/apps/testing/<package>; the Android link uses ?id=<package>.
Paste the logo URL. Show a live preview so mistakes are obvious.
Choose the minimum Android version from a dropdown and store it as an API level.
Publish. The app’s status becomes “open” and it appears in the discover feed.

Also ask testers for their device’s Android version at signup (dropdown), since matching depends on it.

7. Screens
Landing and login (Google Sign-In only)
Onboarding (join group, device Android version)
Dashboard: level, slots used out of total, today’s check-ins due, alerts
My Apps: each app shows a 16-slot progress bar (joined, active, completed)
Discover: cards for open apps with a “Request handshake” button
Requests: incoming and outgoing, with accept/reject
Active Handshakes: one row per app you’re testing, with a daily check-in button, days left and feedback
Profile: level progress, reliability score, block status
Admin: disputes, bans, proof review
8. Suggested stack
Next.js for the frontend
Supabase for auth (Google), Postgres, screenshot storage, and edge functions or cron for the daily job
FCM or email for reminders

This lets one person ship an MVP quickly.

If you’d like, I can write the actual SQL schema with constraints, or build a clickable prototype of the dashboard and handshake flow.